menu "MicroLink V2 Configuration"

    config ML_ZERO_COPY_WG
        bool "Enable zero-copy WireGuard receive (high-throughput mode)"
        default n
        help
            Use raw lwIP UDP PCB instead of BSD sockets for WireGuard packet
            reception on the DISCO UDP socket. This eliminates memory copies
            and reduces latency by injecting WG packets directly into the
            WireGuard stack from the lwIP tcpip_thread.

            When enabled:
            - WireGuard packets: zero-copy direct injection (no malloc, no memcpy)
            - DISCO packets: lock-free SPSC ring buffer to wg_mgr task
            - STUN packets: still handled via BSD socket + queue

            Enable this for high-throughput applications like video streaming
            (30fps+). For typical IoT/sensor applications (heartbeats, commands),
            the default BSD socket mode is simpler and sufficient.

            Zero-copy mode contributed by dj-oyu (https://github.com/dj-oyu/microlink)

    config ML_MAX_PEERS
        int "Maximum simultaneous WireGuard peers"
        default 16
        range 1 64
        help
            Maximum number of peers with active WireGuard tunnels. Each peer
            uses approximately 200 bytes plus endpoint storage.

            Most deployments need 1-4 peers (only the peers you actively
            communicate with). For large tailnets, set this to the actual
            peer count needed, not the total tailnet size.

    config ML_DERP_VERIFY_CERT
        bool "Verify DERP relay TLS certificates"
        depends on MBEDTLS_CERTIFICATE_BUNDLE
        default y
        help
            Check each DERP relay's TLS certificate against ESP-IDF's CA
            bundle and the relay's host name. Tailscale's relays use Let's
            Encrypt certificates, which the bundle covers. WireGuard already
            encrypts relayed traffic; this stops a network in the middle from
            posing as the relay and silently dropping or delaying it.
            Turn off only for a self-hosted DERP server with a private
            certificate.

            This option needs CONFIG_MBEDTLS_CERTIFICATE_BUNDLE. Without the
            bundle it is hidden and relay certificates are NOT checked; the
            build and the first DERP connection both print a warning.

    config ML_DERP_REGION
        int "Preferred DERP region ID"
        default 9
        help
            Tailscale DERP region ID to advertise as this node's home region
            (sent as Hostinfo.NetInfo.PreferredDERP) and to prefer for STUN
            probing/relay fallback before any live latency measurement has
            happened. Defaults to 9 (Dallas, Texas) - fine for North America,
            but every deployment relying on DERP-relayed traffic (e.g. a peer
            behind CGNAT with no direct path) from elsewhere in the world pays
            needless latency relaying through Dallas instead of a nearby
            region. Real Tailscale clients pick this by STUN-probing several
            regions at startup and using whichever has the lowest measured
            latency; this project doesn't do that yet, so pick the closest
            region to this device's usual network by hand instead. Region IDs
            are visible in this device's own boot log (`ml_coord: DERP region
            N (code): ...`, printed once the initial MapResponse is parsed) -
            e.g. 4 = Frankfurt, 14 = Amsterdam, 18 = Paris.

    config ML_PRIORITY_PEER_IP
        string "Priority peer VPN IP"
        default ""
        help
            Tailscale VPN IP of the priority peer (e.g., "100.81.222.123").
            This peer is guaranteed a WireGuard slot even when the peer table
            is full — the least-recently-used non-priority peer is evicted to
            make room.

            On large tailnets where the NVS cache fills the peer table at boot
            before the priority peer arrives from MapResponse, this ensures
            the peer you care about always gets a slot.

            Can also be set at runtime via the HTTP config server web UI.
            Leave empty to disable (all peers treated equally).

    config ML_NVS_MAX_PEERS
        int "Maximum cached peers in NVS"
        default 64
        range 16 1024
        help
            Maximum number of peers stored in the NVS flash cache. Peers are
            persisted across reboots so DISCO probing can start immediately
            before control plane registration completes.

            Storage is a single packed blob: each peer uses 92 bytes.
            When the cache is full, the least-recently-used peer is evicted.

            Examples:
            - 16:   ~1.5KB NVS blob (small IoT deployments)
            - 64:   ~5.9KB NVS blob (default, most tailnets)
            - 256:  ~23.6KB NVS blob (large tailnets)
            - 1024: ~94.2KB NVS blob (enterprise, requires large NVS partition)

            The blob is PSRAM-backed in memory. Ensure your NVS partition is
            large enough for the configured value.

    config ML_H2_BUFFER_SIZE_KB
        int "HTTP/2 receive buffer size (KB)"
        default 512
        range 64 2048
        help
            Size of the PSRAM-allocated buffer for receiving HTTP/2 frames
            from the Tailscale control plane (MapResponse).

            The initial MapResponse contains all peers in your tailnet.
            Each peer is roughly 1-2KB of JSON. After the initial response,
            delta updates (PeersChanged/PeersRemoved) are much smaller.

            - 64:   ~30 peers (small tailnets)
            - 512:  ~300 peers (default, most deployments)
            - 1024: ~600 peers (large enterprise tailnets)
            - 2048: ~1200 peers (very large tailnets, requires 8MB PSRAM)

    config ML_JSON_BUFFER_SIZE_KB
        int "JSON parse buffer size (KB)"
        default 512
        range 64 2048
        help
            Size of the PSRAM-allocated buffer for parsing JSON MapResponse
            data. cJSON builds an in-memory DOM tree that uses 2-3x the
            raw JSON size, so this should be at least as large as the
            HTTP/2 buffer.

            Match this to ML_H2_BUFFER_SIZE_KB unless you have a reason
            to set them differently.

    choice ML_PACKET_FILTER
        prompt "Tailnet packet filter (ACLs)"
        default ML_PACKET_FILTER_ENFORCE
        help
            Enforce the tailnet access policy on inbound traffic, like
            Tailscale clients do (MapResponse PacketFilter/PacketFilters).
            New TCP connections and UDP/ICMP packets from peers are delivered
            only if a rule allows them; replies to traffic this device
            started are always allowed.

        config ML_PACKET_FILTER_ENFORCE
            bool "Enforce (drop what the policy doesn't allow)"
        config ML_PACKET_FILTER_LOG
            bool "Log only (count would-be drops, deliver everything)"
        config ML_PACKET_FILTER_OFF
            bool "Off (accept all inbound traffic)"
    endchoice

    config ML_FILTER_MAX_ENTRIES
        int "Maximum packet-filter entries per rule set"
        default 4096
        range 64 65536
        help
            Upper bound on source + destination entries in one rule set.
            A larger policy is rejected and the previous one kept.

    config ML_SUBNET_ROUTER
        bool "Subnet router / exit node"
        default n
        select LWIP_IP_FORWARD
        select LWIP_IPV4_NAPT
        help
            Let the device advertise subnet routes (or 0.0.0.0/0, as an
            exit node) and forward tailnet traffic for them out of its other
            interface (Wi-Fi station or Ethernet). Forwarded traffic leaves
            with the device's own address as source (SNAT/NAPT), so hosts on
            the LAN need no route back to the tailnet. Enables lwIP IP
            forwarding and NAPT.

            The routes come from microlink_config_t.advertise_routes or,
            when that is NULL, ML_ADVERTISE_ROUTES below. They must be
            approved in the admin console (or by autoApprovers) before
            peers use them.

    config ML_ADVERTISE_ROUTES
        string "Advertised routes"
        depends on ML_SUBNET_ROUTER
        default ""
        help
            Prefixes separated by commas, e.g. "192.168.1.0/24" or
            "fd12:3456::/64" (IPv6 needs ML_ROUTER_IPV6). Add "0.0.0.0/0" to
            offer the device as an exit node. Up to 8 of each family.

    config ML_ROUTER_IPV6
        bool "Forward IPv6: exit node and IPv6 subnet routes (NAT66)"
        depends on ML_SUBNET_ROUTER && LWIP_IPV6 && (LWIP_HOOK_IP6_INPUT_DEFAULT || LWIP_HOOK_IP6_INPUT_CUSTOM)
        default y
        select LWIP_IPV6_FORWARD
        select LWIP_IPV6_AUTOCONFIG
        help
            Forward peers' IPv6 for an exit node (which advertises ::/0 next
            to 0.0.0.0/0, as Tailscale needs both) and for IPv6 prefixes in
            ML_ADVERTISE_ROUTES. The device takes its tailnet IPv6 address,
            gets an address on the LAN by SLAAC and masquerades peers'
            traffic behind it (NAT66), as Linux exit nodes and subnet routers
            do. When the LAN has no address that can reach a destination
            (e.g. no IPv6 there at all), the peer gets ICMPv6 "no route" and
            falls back to IPv4 at once.

            Needs lwIP's IPv6 input hook (LWIP_HOOK_IP6_INPUT) set to
            "Default (weak)" or "Custom": MicroLink provides
            lwip_hook_ip6_input(). An application that needs the hook too
            defines microlink_ip6_input_hook() instead (see microlink.h).

    config ML_OTA_ROLLBACK
        bool "Keep an OTA-updated firmware only once it connects"
        depends on BOOTLOADER_APP_ROLLBACK_ENABLE
        default y
        help
            With the bootloader's app rollback enabled, a firmware written by
            OTA is marked valid when MicroLink first reaches CONNECTED. If it
            does not within ML_OTA_CONFIRM_TIMEOUT_S, the device restarts and
            the bootloader returns to the previous firmware, so an update that
            cannot reach the tailnet does not leave the device unreachable.
            Turn off to confirm updates in your own code
            (esp_ota_mark_app_valid_cancel_rollback()).

    config ML_OTA_CONFIRM_TIMEOUT_S
        int "Seconds an updated firmware has to connect"
        depends on ML_OTA_ROLLBACK
        default 300
        range 30 3600

    config ML_UDP_RX_TASK_PRIO
        int "UDP socket receive task priority"
        default 7
        range 1 22
        help
            FreeRTOS priority of the per-socket task that runs the callback
            registered with microlink_udp_set_rx_callback().

            The default (7) matches MicroLink's WireGuard manager and network
            I/O tasks. If your receive callback does heavy or blocking work,
            set this below 5 so it cannot delay the control-plane and DERP
            tasks.

    config ML_STOP_TIMEOUT_MS
        int "Max time microlink_stop() waits for the internal tasks (ms)"
        default 30000
        range 3000 300000
        help
            microlink_stop() asks the internal tasks to exit, shuts down the
            control-plane and DERP sockets so blocking reads return, and then
            waits up to this long for every task to exit. A DNS lookup or a
            TCP connect cannot be interrupted and may take longer; in that
            case microlink_stop() returns ESP_ERR_TIMEOUT without freeing
            anything and can be called again, and microlink_destroy() defers
            the free until the task has exited.

    choice ML_LOG_LEVEL_CHOICE
        prompt "MicroLink log verbosity (compile time)"
        default ML_LOG_LEVEL_DEFAULT
        help
            Highest level of MicroLink's own log messages (the microlink and
            wireguard_lwip components) that is compiled into the firmware.
            Messages above it are removed by the compiler together with their
            strings. ESP-IDF's own log levels are not changed.

            - Same as ESP-IDF: follow CONFIG_LOG_MAXIMUM_LEVEL (normally the
              default log level, INFO in the examples).
            - Warning: drop all INFO messages; saves roughly 17-23 KB of flash
              in the examples.
            - Info: state changes (connected, peers added/removed, DERP up/down,
              path switched) even if ESP-IDF itself is built quieter.
            - Debug / Verbose: also compile in per-packet and periodic messages
              (Verbose adds the per-packet WireGuard trace, tag "wg"). They are
              still filtered at run time by each tag's level, which starts at
              CONFIG_LOG_DEFAULT_LEVEL, so also call for example
              esp_log_level_set("ml_wg_mgr", ESP_LOG_DEBUG).

        config ML_LOG_LEVEL_DEFAULT
            bool "Same as ESP-IDF"
        config ML_LOG_LEVEL_WARN
            bool "Warning"
        config ML_LOG_LEVEL_INFO
            bool "Info"
        config ML_LOG_LEVEL_DEBUG
            bool "Debug"
        config ML_LOG_LEVEL_VERBOSE
            bool "Verbose"
    endchoice

    config ML_LOG_LEVEL
        int
        default 2 if ML_LOG_LEVEL_WARN
        default 3 if ML_LOG_LEVEL_INFO
        default 4 if ML_LOG_LEVEL_DEBUG
        default 5 if ML_LOG_LEVEL_VERBOSE
        default 3
        help
            esp_log_level_t value used as LOG_LOCAL_LEVEL for MicroLink's
            sources. Not used with ML_LOG_LEVEL_DEFAULT.

    menu "Cellular Modem"

        config ML_ENABLE_CELLULAR
            bool "Enable cellular modem support"
            default n
            help
                Enable 4G cellular modem driver for MicroLink.
                Supports SIMCOM SIM7600 and SIM7670 series modems via UART
                AT socket bridge (AT+NETOPEN/CIPOPEN/CIPSEND).

                When enabled, MicroLink can operate over cellular data instead
                of (or in addition to) WiFi. All Tailscale protocol operations
                work unchanged — traffic is routed through DERP relay.

        choice ML_CELLULAR_BOARD
            prompt "Cellular board type"
            depends on ML_ENABLE_CELLULAR
            default ML_BOARD_WAVESHARE_SIM7600

            config ML_BOARD_WAVESHARE_SIM7600
                bool "Waveshare SIM7600X + XIAO ESP32S3"
                help
                    Waveshare SIM7600X 4G Module connected to Seeed XIAO ESP32S3.
                    Modem UART: TX=GPIO43, RX=GPIO44.
                    No power control pin needed (always on via USB-C).

            config ML_BOARD_LILYGO_T_SIM7670G
                bool "LILYGO T-SIM7670G-S3"
                help
                    LILYGO T-SIM7670G-S3 all-in-one board with integrated SIM7670G modem.
                    Modem UART: TX=GPIO11, RX=GPIO10.
                    Power control: PWRKEY=GPIO18, DTR=GPIO9, RESET=GPIO17.

            config ML_BOARD_CUSTOM
                bool "Custom pin configuration"
                help
                    Manually configure UART and power control GPIO pins.
        endchoice

        config ML_CELLULAR_TX_PIN
            int "Cellular UART TX GPIO pin"
            depends on ML_ENABLE_CELLULAR
            default 11 if ML_BOARD_LILYGO_T_SIM7670G
            default 43
            help
                GPIO pin for ESP32 TX -> modem RXD.
                Waveshare SIM7600X: GPIO43 (XIAO D6).
                LILYGO T-SIM7670G-S3: GPIO11.

        config ML_CELLULAR_REG_TIMEOUT_S
            int "Network registration timeout (seconds)"
            depends on ML_ENABLE_CELLULAR
            default 180
            range 30 900
            help
                How long to wait for the modem to register on the network.
                A cold LTE attach (new SIM, new location, after a power cycle)
                often takes 1-2 minutes. While waiting, signal and
                registration status are logged every ~10 s.

        config ML_CELLULAR_FAST_BAUD
            int "Switch modem UART to this baud after init (0 = keep initial baud)"
            depends on ML_ENABLE_CELLULAR && !ML_BOARD_LILYGO_T_SIM7670G
            default 0
            help
                After the modem answers at the initial baud rate, switch both
                sides to this rate with AT+IPR (e.g. 460800 or 921600) for
                faster PPP. Only reliable with short wiring and/or hardware
                flow control; on failure the driver reverts. The modem stores
                AT+IPR, and the driver normalises it back on the next boot.

        config ML_CELLULAR_RX_PIN
            int "Cellular UART RX GPIO pin"
            depends on ML_ENABLE_CELLULAR
            default 10 if ML_BOARD_LILYGO_T_SIM7670G
            default 44
            help
                GPIO pin for ESP32 RX <- modem TXD.
                Waveshare SIM7600X: GPIO44 (XIAO D7).
                LILYGO T-SIM7670G-S3: GPIO10.

        config ML_CELLULAR_PWRKEY_PIN
            int "Modem PWRKEY GPIO pin"
            depends on ML_ENABLE_CELLULAR
            default 18 if ML_BOARD_LILYGO_T_SIM7670G
            default -1
            help
                GPIO pin for modem power key control.
                Set to -1 if not connected (Waveshare: always-on via USB).
                LILYGO T-SIM7670G-S3: GPIO18.

        config ML_CELLULAR_DTR_PIN
            int "Modem DTR GPIO pin"
            depends on ML_ENABLE_CELLULAR
            default 9 if ML_BOARD_LILYGO_T_SIM7670G
            default -1
            help
                GPIO pin for modem Data Terminal Ready signal.
                Set to -1 if not connected.
                LILYGO T-SIM7670G-S3: GPIO9.

        config ML_CELLULAR_APN
            string "Cellular APN"
            depends on ML_ENABLE_CELLULAR
            default ""
            help
                Access Point Name for cellular data connection.
                Leave empty for auto-detection.
                Letters, digits, '-' and '.' only (3GPP TS 23.003); other
                values are rejected at startup.

        config ML_CELLULAR_SIM_PIN
            string "SIM Card PIN"
            depends on ML_ENABLE_CELLULAR
            default ""
            help
                PIN code for the SIM card. Leave empty if no PIN required.
                Must be 4-8 digits.

                The PIN is only sent when AT+SPIC reports at least 2 attempts
                left (or, if the modem cannot report them, once per boot),
                and never again after a rejection until reboot, so a wrong
                PIN cannot PUK-lock the SIM through retries.

        config ML_CELLULAR_PPP_USER
            string "PPP CHAP username"
            depends on ML_ENABLE_CELLULAR
            default ""
            help
                Username for PPP CHAP authentication.
                Leave empty for SIMs that use IMSI-based auth (no credentials).
                Soracom SIMs use "sora".
                Printable ASCII without '"' or '\', max 64 characters.

        config ML_CELLULAR_PPP_PASS
            string "PPP CHAP password"
            depends on ML_ENABLE_CELLULAR
            default ""
            help
                Password for PPP CHAP authentication.
                Leave empty for SIMs that use IMSI-based auth (no credentials).
                Soracom SIMs use "sora".
                Printable ASCII without '"' or '\', max 64 characters.

        choice ML_CELLULAR_DATA_MODE
            prompt "Cellular data mode"
            depends on ML_ENABLE_CELLULAR
            default ML_CELLULAR_DATA_AUTO
            help
                Select how the modem provides data connectivity.

                Auto: Try PPP first, fall back to AT socket bridge if PPP fails.
                PPP only: Use PPP exclusively. Fails if PPP auth is rejected.
                AT socket only: Skip PPP, use AT socket bridge. The UART stays
                in AT command mode, so the application can send its own AT
                commands (e.g. GPS queries) alongside the data connection with
                ml_cellular_send_at_cmd(). While the bridge is active only
                allowlisted query/information and GNSS commands are accepted
                (AT+CSQ, AT+CPSI?, AT+CREG?, AT+CCLK?, AT+CBC, AT+CGPS=1,
                AT+CGPSINFO, AT+CGNSSINFO, ...; see ml_at_validate.c).
                The bridge is much slower than PPP (seconds of latency); with
                PPP, use the modem's second UART/USB port for GNSS instead.

            config ML_CELLULAR_DATA_AUTO
                bool "Auto (PPP primary, AT socket fallback)"
            config ML_CELLULAR_DATA_PPP_ONLY
                bool "PPP only"
            config ML_CELLULAR_DATA_AT_ONLY
                bool "AT socket only"
        endchoice

    endmenu

    menu "Network Switching"

        config ML_ENABLE_NET_SWITCH
            bool "Enable WiFi/Cellular network switching"
            depends on ML_ENABLE_CELLULAR
            default n
            help
                Automatic WiFi-primary with cellular fallback.
                When WiFi connection fails or health check detects loss,
                switches to cellular. Periodically checks if WiFi has
                recovered and fails back.

        config ML_NET_SWITCH_WIFI_TIMEOUT_MS
            int "WiFi connect timeout (ms)"
            depends on ML_ENABLE_NET_SWITCH
            default 30000
            range 1000 600000
            help
                How long to wait for WiFi connection before falling back
                to cellular. Used when ml_net_switch_config_t.wifi_timeout_ms
                is 0.

        config ML_NET_SWITCH_HEALTH_INTERVAL_MS
            int "Health check interval (ms)"
            depends on ML_ENABLE_NET_SWITCH
            default 30000
            range 1000 3600000
            help
                Interval between health checks when connected.
                After 3 consecutive failures, triggers network switch.
                Used when ml_net_switch_config_t.health_check_interval_ms is 0.

        config ML_NET_SWITCH_FAILBACK_MS
            int "WiFi failback check interval (ms)"
            depends on ML_ENABLE_NET_SWITCH
            default 120000
            range 0 3600000
            help
                When on cellular, how often to check if WiFi has recovered.
                Set to 0 to disable automatic failback. Used when
                ml_net_switch_config_t.failback_check_ms is 0.

    endmenu

    menu "Credentials"
        comment "Configure via: idf.py menuconfig → MicroLink V2 → Credentials"
        comment "These values are stored in sdkconfig (git-ignored)."

        config ML_WIFI_SSID
            string "WiFi SSID"
            default ""
            help
                SSID of the WiFi network to connect to.
                This is stored in sdkconfig which should be git-ignored.

        config ML_WIFI_PASSWORD
            string "WiFi Password"
            default ""
            help
                Password for the WiFi network.
                This is stored in sdkconfig which should be git-ignored.

        config ML_TAILSCALE_AUTH_KEY
            string "Tailscale Auth Key"
            default ""
            help
                Tailscale authentication key from https://login.tailscale.com/admin/settings/keys
                Generate a reusable + ephemeral key for development.
                This is stored in sdkconfig which should be git-ignored.
                Leave empty to join by interactive login instead: the device
                gets a login URL from the control plane (ML_STATE_NEEDS_LOGIN,
                microlink_get_auth_url(); basic_connect prints it as a QR code).

        config ML_DEVICE_NAME
            string "Device Name"
            default ""
            help
                Hostname for this device on the Tailscale network.
                Leave empty for auto-generated name (esp32-XXYYZZ from MAC,
                or prefix-IMEI from cellular modem).

    endmenu

    menu "Control Plane"

        config ML_CTRL_HOST
            string "Control plane hostname"
            default "controlplane.tailscale.com"
            help
                Coordination server hostname. Set to a Headscale-compatible
                host (e.g. Lolipop Zero Trust Link controller) to use a
                custom control plane. NVS runtime override still wins.

        config ML_CTRL_TLS
            bool "Connect to control plane over TLS (port 443)"
            default n
            help
                Wrap the ts2021 Noise handshake in TLS on port 443 instead
                of plain TCP on port 80. Required for control planes that
                only expose an HTTPS listener (e.g. Headscale behind an LB).
                The control plane itself is authenticated by the pinned Noise
                public key; see ML_CTRL_TLS_VERIFY_CERT for the TLS layer.

        config ML_CTRL_TLS_VERIFY_CERT
            bool "Verify the control plane's TLS certificate"
            depends on ML_CTRL_TLS && MBEDTLS_CERTIFICATE_BUNDLE
            default y
            help
                Check the control plane's TLS certificate against ESP-IDF's
                CA bundle and its host name. Noise already authenticates the
                server; this additionally stops a network in the middle from
                posing as the HTTPS endpoint. Turn off only for a self-hosted
                server with a private/self-signed certificate.

                This option needs CONFIG_MBEDTLS_CERTIFICATE_BUNDLE. Without
                the bundle it is hidden and the certificate is NOT checked;
                the build and each TLS handshake print a warning.

        config ML_CTRL_STREAMING_MAP_FETCH
            bool "Fetch the initial netmap over the streaming map request"
            default n
            help
                Use a single Stream=true map request for the initial netmap
                (like the official client) instead of a Stream=false request
                followed by a long-poll. Needed for Headscale, which does not
                answer Stream=false; MicroLink also switches automatically if
                the server sends an empty response. Leave off for Tailscale
                and Ionscale.

        config ML_CTRL_NOISE_PUBKEY_HEX
            string "Control plane Noise public key (hex)"
            default ""
            help
                64-char hex of the control plane's Noise (machine) public key,
                from https://<control-host>/key?v=131 → "publicKey":"mkey:<hex>".
                Empty = use the built-in Tailscale key.

    endmenu

    menu "HTTP Config Server"

        config ML_ENABLE_CONFIG_HTTPD
            bool "Enable HTTP configuration server"
            default n
            help
                Enable an HTTP server accessible via the Tailscale VPN IP
                (e.g., http://100.x.x.x/) for runtime configuration of
                WiFi credentials, Tailscale auth key, peer allowlist, etc.

                Every request requires HTTP Basic auth (user "admin") and is
                only served on the device's tailnet (VPN) address unless
                ML_CONFIG_HTTPD_ALLOW_LAN is set. Anyone with the password
                can change the auth key and network settings.

                Settings persist in NVS across reboots. The peer allowlist
                controls which peers receive outbound DISCO probes — on large
                tailnets this prevents wg_mgr flooding and reduces latency.

        config ML_CONFIG_HTTPD_OTA
            bool "Firmware upload (POST /api/ota)"
            depends on ML_ENABLE_CONFIG_HTTPD
            default n
            help
                Accept a new app image (the .bin from the build) with
                POST /api/ota, under the same access rules as the rest of the
                config server, write it to the next OTA slot and restart into
                it. The image is checked before it is used (chip, SHA-256
                digest, and its signature with secure boot). Needs a partition
                table with two OTA slots. tools/ml_ota.py updates a list of
                devices this way. Enable BOOTLOADER_APP_ROLLBACK_ENABLE too, so
                that an update that cannot reconnect is rolled back
                (ML_OTA_ROLLBACK).

        config ML_CONFIG_HTTPD_PASSWORD
            string "Admin password (empty = generate on first boot)"
            depends on ML_ENABLE_CONFIG_HTTPD
            default ""
            help
                Password for the config UI (HTTP Basic auth, user "admin").
                Leave empty to generate a random password on first start; it
                is stored in NVS and printed once on the serial console.
                Note: a password set here is compiled into the firmware.

        config ML_CONFIG_HTTPD_ALLOW_LAN
            bool "Also serve the config UI on the local (WiFi/Ethernet) network"
            depends on ML_ENABLE_CONFIG_HTTPD
            default n
            help
                By default the config UI only answers on the device's own
                tailnet (VPN) address, where traffic is WireGuard-encrypted.
                Enable to also answer on the WiFi station / Ethernet
                interface's own address; Basic auth is then sent in cleartext
                on that LAN. Never served on the cellular (PPP) carrier
                address.

        config ML_CONFIG_MAX_ALLOWED_PEERS
            int "Maximum peers in allowlist"
            depends on ML_ENABLE_CONFIG_HTTPD
            default 16
            range 1 1024
            help
                Maximum number of peers in the DISCO probe allowlist.
                Each entry uses 28 bytes in NVS (IP + 24-char label).
                ESP-IDF NVS supports multi-page blobs up to 508KB,
                so large allowlists are not a problem.

                - 16:    448 bytes (default, small deployments)
                - 128:   3.6KB
                - 300:   8.4KB (recommended for large fleets)
                - 1024:  28.7KB

                When the allowlist is empty, all peers are probed (backwards
                compatible). When populated, only listed peers receive
                outbound DISCO probes and WG peer table slots.

    endmenu

endmenu
